WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.700
In the last year, North Korea has been launching a lot of missiles.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.880
They're all just tests, but they're meant to send a message.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.460
North Korea is armed and dangerous.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.160
They've demonstrated missiles that can destroy Seoul and Tokyo.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.860
Hit US bases in the Pacific.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.860
And now one that can reach Alaska.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.880
US officials have confirmed that over night North Korea has launched

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.880
an intercontinental ballistic missile,

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.880
an ICBM.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.240
Capable of reaching Alaska.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.800
Kim Jung Un called it a gift to the Trump Administration.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.100
This is the end goal or at least towards the end goal for North Korea.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.840
It’s called the Hwasong-14. it’s the newest member of North Korea’s arsenal, and their

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:42.660
first working Intercontinental ballistic missile, or ICBM.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.570
It's the kind of thing that the US and
Russia have thousands of, and had used as

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:52.810
a threat against each other during the Cold War.

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:57.109
North Korea has been working on one of its own for a while and they now appear to have one.

00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:01.850
The North Koreans have had nuclear bombs since
2006, and they could certainly attack their

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:06.020
neighbors, but they’ve had no real way to
deliver them to a distant target.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:09.380
They could try and drop one from an airplane, but it would likely be shot down well before

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.120
getting to the target.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.930
The most effective way to use a nuclear bomb is to attach it to a missile.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:18.200
Different delivery systems determine how far the target can be.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.540
North Korea already has a huge arsenal of
short and medium range missiles.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:26.830
But it DIDN’T have a working ICBM, which
can hit a target up to 15,000 kilometers away

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:30.210
because it’s actually multiple missiles
in 1.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:34.400
Stacked on top of each other, each missile
is used one-by-one to push the warhead into

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:35.400
space.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.150
Then once it’s over a target, this final
4th section re-enters the atmosphere and detonates.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:45.550
This is a very hard thing to engineer and
tests of a previous long-range missile, the

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:47.140
Taepodong-2, failed repeatedly.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:52.960
BUT ON JULY 4, 2017, North Korea successfully tested an ICBM called the Hwasong-14, a missile

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:53.960
capable of reaching Alaska.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:59.720
It reached a distance of about 930 kilometers, flew for nearly 40 minutes, and successfully,

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.690
re-entered, splashing down in the Sea of Japan.

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.000
This is a major milestone for North Korea.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.800
Analysts calculate that if North Korea wanted
to use the Hwasong-14 in a real attack, they

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.670
would launch it at a lower angle, giving it
a range of more than 5,500km, putting Alaska

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:14.850
in range.

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:18.200
But this still doesn’t mean North Korea
can nuke Alaska today.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:22.920
The Pentagon says the North Koreans cannot fit a nuclear bomb on the Hwasong-14 and have

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:23.920
it reliably detonate.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.500
The Hwasong-14 is a relatively small ICBM, meaning it can’t carry a very heavy nuclear

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:29.500
bomb.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.340
In order for it to make it to Alaska, the
North Koreans would have to figure out how

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.420
to “miniaturize” the bomb.
And there’s no evidence the North Koreans can

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:36.420
do this yet.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.120
But the Hwasong-14 test is still very serious, because it shows that North Korea is rapidly

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.770
improving its ICBM technology and that it’s determined to build one, no matter who tries

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:45.010
to stop them.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:50.290
So, this raises the question - Why is North
Korea so dead set on building an ICBM?

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:55.260
The North Koreans first got nuclear technology in the 1950s, when their ally, the Soviet

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.820
Union, helped them build nuclear reactors
for energy.

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:02.180
At the time, they were protected from their enemies, South Korea and the US, by the massive

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.650
nuclear arsenal of the Soviet Union.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:08.570
But in 1993, when the Soviet Union collapsed, that protection went away.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:11.930
So, North Korean dictator Kim Sung Il / Kim Jung il took matters into his own hands and

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.960
started using the nuclear reactors to make
weapons.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.650
The US didn’t want an aggressive, rogue
state to have nuclear weapons, so 3 consecutive

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:23.100
US presidents all tried different tactics
to get north korea to stop.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:23.600
Clinton negotiated, Bush suspended negotiations and threatened them, and

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:27.709
Obama simply tried

00:03:27.709 --> 00:03:29.060
to wait North Korea out.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:33.600
But every single plan failed to stop the nuclear
program — and there’s a reason for that.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.990
See, the Kim dynasty believes their only option for security is to have proven nuclear capabilities.

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:43.120
They saw the US invade Iraq and topple Saddam Hussein because they thought he might have

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:44.120
nukes.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.010
They also saw Libyan dictator Muammar Gaddafi negotiate with the US and give up his nuclear

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:50.850
program, only to be killed by US-backed rebels.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.600
The Kim dynasty is determined not to be next.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.100
So they’ve gone on to build an arsenal of
missiles for their nuclear bombs.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.569
The No Dong is functional today and has a range of 1,500km.

00:04:00.569 --> 00:04:06.170
And North Korea’s SCUD missiles are highly effective within 1,000 km.

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:11.230
This puts North Korea’s main enemies, South Korea and Japan within range as well as the

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:12.910
62,000 US troops stationed there.

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:15.819
In fact, North Korea wants to make that loud and clear.

00:04:15.819 --> 00:04:19.870
And it does so by successfully testing these short range missiles - a lot.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:25.761
The tests provide a chance for them to improve their missiles but more importantly, they

00:04:25.761 --> 00:04:27.500
send a political message.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.040
South Korea and Japan wouldn’t attack North Korea because they’ve seen the missiles

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.450
that could devastate some of their largest
cities.

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:36.710
Also…they don’t have any nuclear weapons of their own.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:38.690
So what’s keeping North Korea from attacking its sworn enemies?

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:44.630
The US - which has promised to protect South Korea and Japan with the largest ICBM arsenal

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:45.730
in the world.

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.810
This puts North Korea at a disadvantage.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:51.980
It knows the US can obliterate it, but it
can’t reach the US just yet.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:55.850
This is why the US can safely to cut off North Korean trade and enact other punishments.

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:59.990
And this brings us back to why North Korea has been trying so hard to build an ICBM;

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.490
it’s what they need to tip the scales in
their favor.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:07.320
Right now, North Korea can only attack US allies but if it can successfully test any

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:12.520
of these ICBMs, it would be the same as telling the US “if you nuke us, we’ll nuke you”.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.500
If the US knows North Korea can target an
American city, it‘ll be less likely to come

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:20.530
to the aid of South Korea or Japan This would effectively break up the alliance that’s

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:22.470
designed to keep North Korea in check.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:26.280
But the implications of this would go far
beyond the Korean peninsula.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:30.230
Suddenly in charge of their own protection, South Korea and Japan would potentially develop

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:34.960
nuclear weapons of their own -- something Donald Trump once said he would be fine with.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:38.680
This would trigger a process called nuclear proliferation and it’s exactly what the

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:40.919
US has been trying to prevent since the Cold War.

00:05:40.919 --> 00:05:44.690
Right now, there are 9 countries with known nuclear weapons of their own, while 190 states

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:49.090
that maintain a non-nuclear status because of the Non-proliferation treaty.

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:52.280
But the success of a North Korean ICBM could change that.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.290
If South Korea and Japan start looking to
change their nuclear status, it could encourage

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:58.360
other countries to do the same.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:02.280
So experts are urging the US to take action now while it still can.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:03.910
President Trump is pressing China to help.

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:08.190
See, China continues to trade and have a diplomatic relationship with North Korea, essentially

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:09.780
keeping the regime alive.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:13.730
So if Trump can get China to leverage or cut off that support, North Korea might decide

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:17.550
that it’s not worth continuing to pour money into developing better ICBMs.

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:22.060
But for now, as North Korea continues to test and parade missiles, tensions continue to

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:23.320
rise.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:27.380
Three US presidents have said they’d prevent the North from building better nukes and missiles.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:28.919
Three US presidents have failed.

00:06:28.919 --> 00:06:42.520
At least for now, it looks like Donald Trump will be the fourth.

